{"causes": ["The relentless military expansion and imperial ambitions of the Neo-Assyrian Empire created immense pressure on independent Levantine city-states.", "Tyre's strategic location and immense commercial wealth made it a highly desirable target for Assyrian economic and political control.", "The inherent military disadvantage of small, though prosperous, Phoenician city-states against a vast, organized imperial army necessitated pragmatic diplomatic solutions."], "effects": ["Ithobaal II's submission solidified Tyre's position as a tributary state within the Neo-Assyrian Empire's vast economic and political sphere.", "The precedent of paying tribute under Ithobaal II ensured the continuation of Tyre's semi-autonomous status under his successor, Hiram II, for several more decades.", "His actions likely averted a destructive Assyrian siege against Tyre, preserving the city's infrastructure and commercial networks."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "tiglat-pileser-iii", "sourceName": "Tiglath-pileser III", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "ithobaal-ii", "targetName": "Ithobaal II", "context": "Tiglath-pileser III's demands for tribute directly influenced Ithobaal II's decision to submit and pay in 738 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "ithobaal-ii", "sourceName": "Ithobaal II", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "tyre", "targetName": "Tyre", "context": "Ithobaal II ruled as king from the powerful Phoenician city-state of Tyre during the 8th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "hiram-ii", "sourceName": "Hiram II", "verb": "SUCCEEDS", "targetSlug": "ithobaal-ii", "targetName": "Ithobaal II", "context": "Hiram II succeeded Ithobaal II as king of Tyre in 738 or 737 BCE, continuing the policy of tribute to Assyria."}, {"sourceSlug": "ithobaal-ii", "sourceName": "Ithobaal II", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "assyrian-tribute-system", "targetName": "Assyrian Tribute System", "context": "Ithobaal II's payment of tribute in 738 BCE directly integrated Tyre into the broader Assyrian imperial economic and political structure."}, {"sourceSlug": "neo-assyrian-empire", "sourceName": "Neo-Assyrian Empire", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "tyre", "targetName": "Tyre", "context": "The Neo-Assyrian Empire, through its military dominance and imposition of tribute, effectively contained the city-state of Tyre under Ithobaal II's reign."}], "places": [{"name": "Tyre, Lebanon", "role": "Seat of power"}, {"name": "Nineveh, Iraq", "role": "Assyrian Imperial Capital"}, {"name": "Mediterranean Sea", "role": "Economic sphere"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Ithobaal II", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "ithobaal-ii"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3155928", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ithobaal_II"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 5, "significanceNarrative": "Ithobaal II's reign, though brief, is a crucial marker of Phoenician city-states' submission to the Neo-Assyrian Empire, illustrating the pragmatic choices made by regional powers to maintain their commercial networks and avoid destruction.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-08-17T20:29:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32064130018", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Ithobaal II was an eighth-century BC Phoenician king of Tyre. Nothing is known of his reign except that he paid tribute to the Assyrian king Tiglath-pileser III in 738. He was succeeded that year or the next by Hiram II, who continued the tribute to the Assyrians.", "newValue": "Ithobaal II presided as the Phoenician king of Tyre during a critical juncture in the 8th century BCE, a period defined by the relentless expansion of the Neo-Assyrian Empire across the Levant. His historical footprint, though sparse in indigenous records, is solidified by foreign annals, specifically the Assyrian tribute lists which document his submission to Tiglath-pileser III. This act of fealty, recorded around 738 BCE, underscores the diminishing autonomy of powerful city-states like Tyre in the face of a burgeoning imperial power, positioning Ithobaal II as a figure emblematic of a broader geopolitical shift.

The core event of Ithobaal II's brief documented reign was the payment of tribute to the Assyrian king Tiglath-pileser III. This transaction, likely a substantial sum of silver, gold, and other valuable commodities for which Tyre was renowned, occurred either in 738 BCE or shortly thereafter, marking Tyre's formal incorporation into the Assyrian tributary system. This submission was not an isolated incident but part of a wider Assyrian campaign to subjugate the wealthy coastal cities of Phoenicia, securing their resources and strategic ports for the empire's economic and military objectives. Ithobaal II's decision to pay tribute, rather than resist, likely preserved Tyre's commercial infrastructure and prevented a devastating siege.

The immediate consequence of Ithobaal II's policy was the continuation of Tyre's tributary status under his successor, Hiram II, who assumed the throne either in the same year or the subsequent one. This established a precedent for Tyre's relationship with Assyria for decades to come, ensuring the city's survival as a major trading hub, albeit under the shadow of imperial suzerainty. The economic concessions, while significant, allowed Tyre to retain a degree of internal governance and maintain its vast Mediterranean trade networks, a pragmatic strategy that defined the survival of many smaller states in the Assyrian orbit."}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-17T20:29:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32064130018", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "750 BCE"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "750 BCE"}], "newValue": ["The relentless military expansion and imperial ambitions of the Neo-Assyrian Empire created immense pressure on independent Levantine city-states.", "Tyre's strategic location and immense commercial wealth made it a highly desirable target for Assyrian economic and political control.", "The inherent military disadvantage of small, though prosperous, Phoenician city-states against a vast, organized imperial army necessitated pragmatic diplomatic solutions."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-08-17T20:29:51Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#32064130018", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "730 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "730 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Ithobaal II's submission solidified Tyre's position as a tributary state within the Neo-A